there is no escaping the agony in beirut  hospitals already inundated with victims  of the explosion  are now filling with protesters injured  by the security forces  firas hamden told me he was out on the  streets last saturday  and whilst filming this facebook live  was almost killed  you can see the explosion in the center  of the crowd  a metal pellet from what it's thought  was a grenade pierced his heart  i walked for two meters and i  shut down as i collapsed on the earth  and  the other colleague i'll bring me  to him i am glad to be  alive it pierced all the chest walls the  heart from side to side and it looked  into the posterior  posterior wall of the heart firas  underwent emergency surgery  and amazingly he'll make a full recovery  dr karim neme who's been treating him  says he's seen more than 50 patients  wounded in the protests  many of the injuries are too graphic to  show  all the injuries were on the upper upper  part of the body  face shots neck shots chest injuries  all all of these could kill the patients  could kill the protesters  lebanon's anti-government protests  erupted after the explosion at the port  the human rights activists and doctors  claimed the methods deployed here by the  security services  are in breach of international law  [Music]  rabby barakat bears the scars from the  tear gas canister  it appeared to have been fired directly  at his face  he's come back to the place where it  happened to tell us  what he remembers and i had no idea  whether this was live ammunition rubber  bullet  or um a tear gas bomb i would really  like to  know who uh gave the order to shoot  these  tear gas bombs straight  straight to our heads and i would really  like to  to look him in the eye  and tell him was this really necessary  human rights watch observed these  protests first  hand it's conducted its own  investigation  the evidence is overwhelming security  forces  seem to have fired live ammunition at  protesters they seem to have fired  rubber bullets including a protesters  faces which is prohibited by  international law  the lebanese authorities are denying  they used excessive force  or rubber bullets but in this country  hollowed out by years of corruption  where people have no faith in the  government it's a response that's  unlikely to be believed  alex rossi sky news beirut
 

